<section>
    <h2>One test, five traps</h2>
    <p>
        Real tests rarely check one thing. They sign in, find a record, fill a form, wait for a save, and check what
        came back, all in one run. Every skill from the floors above is in this boss room, in order, and you need all
        of them in a single test. This scroll has nothing new to memorize. It's about putting the pieces together so
        the test stays readable, and finding out what went wrong when it doesn't pass.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Build it one trial at a time</h2>
    <p>
        Don't write the whole test and then run it. Write the first step, run it, and watch it pass. Then add the next.
        When a long test fails, you want to know it was the step you just added.
    </p>
    <p>
        Between steps, wait for the page to show you've moved on. A web-first assertion on the new heading or the new
        form does it, and it doubles as a checkpoint: when it fails, the error tells you which step never finished.
    </p>
<pre><code>await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Save" }).ClickAsync();
await Expect(Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Heading, new() { Name = "Step 2 of 3" })).ToBeVisibleAsync();</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Set your listeners before you act</h2>
    <p>
        Two things from earlier floors only work if they're set up <em>before</em> the action that triggers them:
        a dialog handler, and listening for a response. Dialog handlers can go at the very top of the test, where
        they can't be forgotten. Responses are caught by wrapping the action that sends the request:
    </p>
<pre><code>Page.Dialog += async (_, dialog) =&gt; await dialog.AcceptAsync();

// ... many steps later ...

var response = await Page.RunAndWaitForResponseAsync(
    () =&gt; Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Submit" }).ClickAsync(),
    r =&gt; r.Url.Contains("/api/orders"));</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Keep it readable</h2>
    <p>
        Five steps in one method gets long fast. Give each step a small helper with a name that says what it does, so
        the test reads like the plan:
    </p>
<pre><code>[Test]
public async Task CheckoutWorks()
{
    await SignInAsync();
    await AddToBasketAsync("Lantern");
    await PayAsync();
    await Expect(Page.GetByText("Order confirmed")).ToBeVisibleAsync();
}

private Task SignInAsync() =&gt; ...;</code></pre>
    <p>Locators and values can still pass from one step to the next as arguments and return values.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>When it fails too fast to watch: tracing</h2>
    <p>
        The Inspector is great, until the page has a clock. Stepping line by line takes minutes; the boss gives you
        seconds. Instead, let the test run at full speed and record a <strong>trace</strong>: every action, a snapshot
        of the page before and after it, the network and the console.
    </p>
<pre><code>[SetUp]
public async Task StartTracing() =&gt;
    await Context.Tracing.StartAsync(new() { Screenshots = true, Snapshots = true, Sources = true });

[TearDown]
public async Task StopTracing() =&gt;
    await Context.Tracing.StopAsync(new()
    {
        Path = Path.Combine(TestContext.CurrentContext.WorkDirectory, "trace.zip"),
    });</code></pre>
    <p>
        The file lands in your test project's build folder (<code>bin\Debug\</code>, then your .NET version). Drop it
        on <a href="https://trace.playwright.dev">trace.playwright.dev</a>; the trace is opened in your browser, not
        uploaded. Click any step to see the page exactly as it was, and open the Network tab to see every request and
        its response.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Face the Warden</h2>
    <p>
        Five trials: a gate with a sealed twin, an eye that opens once, an oath, a mirror that asks if you're sure, and a
        name that's only ever whispered. Get one wrong and you start again. Fifteen seconds.
    </p>
</section>
